  "lapsed_date": "16/11/2029",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed House of Multiple Occupation (HMO) would result in a clustering and excessive number of conversions along this section of Nags Head Road, thereby harming the character of the surrounding area, contrary to Chapter 6 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policies H1, H8 and H10 of the London Plan (2021), CP4 and CP5 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD5, DMD6, DMD 8, and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014), the Department for Communities and Local Government Technical housing standards (2015) and the Enfield Strategic Housing Market Assessment (2015).",
    "The proposed development, by reason of insufficient information to confirm internal floor to ceiling heights, would give rise to substandard and inappropriate accommodation, incapable of meeting the reasonable needs of occupiers of this particular bedroom. In the absence of appropriate information to indicate otherwise, the development is thereby contrary to Policies; D3 and D6 of the London Plan, CP 4 and CP 30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD 5, DMD 6, and DMD 8 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014), in addition to the high-quality design aspirations set out within the NPPF (2026).",
    "The proposed cycle parking and refuse storage arrangements, by reason of its siting and insufficient information submitted to adequately assess visual and amenity impacts, would contribute to visual clutter and detract from the character and appearance of the area. The proposal would also fail to provide convenient, safe and accessible cycle parking, with inadequate space available within the front forecourt and no direct access from the rear cycle store to the public realm. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policies D3, D4 and T5 of the London Plan, Policies CP30 and CP36 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD 8, DMD 37, DMD 45 and DMD 47 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014), the London Cycling Design Standards, and the high-quality design objectives of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "The proposed development, in the absence of sufficient information, including a Parking Stress Survey, demonstrating that the level of parking provision and associated highway impacts would be commensurate with the use proposed, is unacceptable and contrary to Section 9 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policies T2, T3, T4 and T6 of the London Plan (2021), Policy CP24 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), and Policies DMD45 and DMD48 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014). In the absence of sufficient evidence to demonstrate otherwise, the proposal would give rise to harmful impacts on street parking congestion and result in conditions prejudicial to the free flow and safety of vehicular traffic and pedestrian movements."
  ],
